{"test_id": "B02-001", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B02", "input": {"question": "A bank's CII implements remote VPN access with username/password plus an SMS one-time code sent to the user's registered mobile number. Does this satisfy CCoP 2.0 Clause 5.1.5 multi-factor authentication requirements?", "scenario_sector": "banking", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "compliant", "expected_response": "This configuration is compliant with CCoP 2.0 Clause 5.7.2(b). It implements two independent authentication factors: something you know (password) and something you have (mobile phone receiving SMS). CCoP 2.0 requires MFA but does not prohibit SMS-based codes or prescribe specific MFA technologies. The requirement is satisfied by combining two independent factors. While NIST recommends stronger MFA than SMS due to SIM swapping risks, and while banking sector best practices may favour app-based or hardware token MFA, the regulatory minimum set by CCoP 2.0 is met. An auditor would not reject this configuration solely based on the SMS factor type, provided the MFA is enforced for all remote access to the CII.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 Clause 5.1.5 requires MFA for remote access to CII systems but does not specify MFA technology type", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 5.1.5", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "SMS one-time code qualifies as a possession factor (something you have) satisfying the second authentication factor requirement", "source": "Regulatory interpretation, multi-factor authentication principles", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "CCoP 2.0 is technology-neutral on MFA implementation — organizations choose methods appropriate to their context", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 5.1.5, RESPONSE-TO-FEEDBACK", "tier": "important"}, {"fact": "Industry best practices (NIST) recommend against SMS-based MFA due to SIM swapping risk, but this does not make SMS non-compliant with CCoP 2.0", "source": "NIST SP 800-63B, Regulatory interpretation", "tier": "supporting"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["SMS-based MFA does not satisfy CCoP 2.0 MFA requirements", "CCoP 2.0 mandates hardware token or app-based MFA only"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Citing a specific CCoP clause that prohibits SMS as an MFA factor", "Claiming NIST standards are incorporated by reference into CCoP 2.0 requirements"]}, "metadata": {"section": "5", "clause_reference": ["5.1.2", "5.7.2"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "low", "scenario_type": "mfa_technology_compliance", "related_sections": ["5.2.1"], "test_category": "positive", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B02-002", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B02", "input": {"question": "A water utility retains security logs for all CII SCADA systems for 18 months. The most recent 6 months are on-premise in hot storage and accessible within seconds. Months 7-18 are in cold archive with a 48-hour retrieval time. Does this comply with CCoP 2.0 Clause 6.1.3?", "scenario_sector": "water", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "compliant", "expected_response": "Re-write to cite §6.1.4(c) for the 12-month retention requirement and §6.1.4(b) for log protection. Drop the unsupported 'most recent 3 months immediately accessible' requirement — CCoP 2.0 does not specify any tier-based access timeline. The 18-month retention exceeds the 12-month minimum (compliant); the cold-archive 48-hour retrieval is not regulated by CCoP 2.0.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 Clause 6.1.3 requires security log retention for at least one year (12 months)", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 6.1.3", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "CCoP 2.0 Clause 6.1.3 requires the most recent three months of logs to be immediately accessible", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 6.1.3", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Retaining logs for 18 months satisfies the 12-month minimum; providing 6 months of immediate access satisfies the 3-month immediate access requirement", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 6.1.3", "tier": "important"}, {"fact": "Cold archive with 48-hour retrieval is acceptable for logs older than three months under Clause 6.1.3", "source": "Regulatory interpretation", "tier": "supporting"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Cold storage with 48-hour retrieval violates CCoP 2.0 log retention requirements", "All 18 months of logs must be immediately accessible to satisfy CCoP 2.0"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Claiming CCoP 2.0 specifies a specific maximum retrieval time for archived logs", "Inventing a higher retention requirement for OT/SCADA systems"]}, "metadata": {"section": "Section 6: Detection", "clause_reference": ["6.1.4"], "domain": "OT", "difficulty": "low", "scenario_type": "log_retention_compliance", "related_sections": ["6.1"], "test_category": "positive", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B02-003", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B02", "input": {"question": "An energy company's CII receives a critical security patch on January 1 for a vulnerability with active exploitation in the wild. The patch is tested in an isolated lab environment and deployed to production SCADA systems on January 13 — 13 days after vendor release. Does this comply with CCoP 2.0 Clause 5.6.4?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "compliant", "expected_response": "This configuration is compliant with CCoP 2.0 Clause 5.10.1(e), which requires security patches to be applied in a timely manner to reduce cybersecurity vulnerabilities, and Clause 5.10.1(d), which requires prioritising patch application based on the level of risk posed to the operations of the CII. A prompt deployment (within roughly two weeks) of a critical patch addressing a vulnerability with active exploitation or publicly available exploit code — including a short testing phase — reflects appropriate risk-based prioritisation and timely application. Clause 5.10.1(c) expects patches to be tested in an environment similar to the CII production environment before deployment; the testing period described does not compromise compliance. In the OT context, testing patches in an isolated lab before deploying to production SCADA is good practice. CCoP 2.0 does not prescribe a fixed number of days — 'timely' is a risk-based determination — and the documented process meets that standard.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 Clause 5.6.4 requires critical patches (actively exploited vulnerabilities) to be applied within 14 days (2 weeks) of vendor release", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 5.6.4", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Deploying the patch 13 days after vendor release is within the 14-day compliance window", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 5.6.4", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Testing the patch in an isolated lab before production deployment is appropriate practice and does not create a compliance issue within the 14-day window", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 5.6, Regulatory interpretation", "tier": "important"}, {"fact": "The 14-day timeline applies to OT/SCADA systems as well as IT systems — Section 10 provides implementation flexibility but does not change the timeline", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 5.6.4, Section 10", "tier": "supporting"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["OT/SCADA systems have a longer patch timeline than 14 days for critical patches", "Testing time is excluded from the 14-day patch timeline"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Claiming CCoP 2.0 specifies a 21-day or 30-day critical patch window", "Inventing an OT-specific exception to the 14-day critical patch requirement"]}, "metadata": {"section": "5", "clause_reference": ["5.10.1(e)"], "domain": "OT", "difficulty": "low", "scenario_type": "patch_timeline_compliance", "related_sections": ["5.6", "10.1.1"], "test_category": "positive", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B02-004", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B02", "input": {"question": "A healthcare CII's incident response plan was last tested via tabletop exercise in March 2023. The next test is scheduled for September 2024 — an 18-month interval. As of October 2024, the September test was completed as planned. Does this comply with CCoP 2.0 Clause 7.1.2?", "scenario_sector": "healthcare", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "non-compliant", "expected_response": "Rewrite to cite §7.1.6 for the annual IRP review cadence and §7.3.1 for the obligation to conduct scenario-based exercises that test IRP effectiveness. Avoid asserting a generic 'IRP must be tested at least annually' rule — CCoP 2.0 mandates an annual *review* (§7.1.6); §7.3.1 requires scenario-based exercises but does not state an explicit annual frequency.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 Clause 7.1.2 requires incident response plans to be tested at least annually (every 12 months)", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 7.1.2", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "An 18-month interval between tests (March 2023 to September 2024) exceeds the 12-month maximum and constitutes non-compliance", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 7.1.2", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "The next test should have been conducted by March 2024 — completing it in September 2024 remediated the breach but does not eliminate the prior non-compliance period", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 7.1.2", "tier": "important"}, {"fact": "Tabletop exercises satisfy the testing requirement under Clause 7.1.2 — the issue is frequency, not exercise type", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 7.1.2", "tier": "supporting"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["An 18-month testing interval complies with CCoP 2.0 incident response testing requirements", "Completing the September 2024 test retroactively satisfies the March 2024 deadline"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Claiming CCoP 2.0 allows biennial IRP testing", "Inventing a grace period for the first test under CCoP 2.0"]}, "metadata": {"section": "Section 7: Response and Recovery", "clause_reference": ["7.1.6", "7.3.1"], "domain": "IT/OT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "irp_testing_frequency", "related_sections": ["7.1"], "test_category": "negative", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B02-005", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B02", "input": {"question": "An energy company separates its OT network from the IT network using a next-generation firewall with deep packet inspection, application-aware filtering, and intrusion prevention system capabilities. OT and IT are in separate VLANs with the firewall enforcing access controls between them. Does this satisfy CCoP 2.0 Clause 10.2.3?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "compliant", "expected_response": "Rewrite to evaluate compliance against §10.2.1 (OT CII shall not be connected to enterprise network except where necessary, with data flows restricted to one-way OT→enterprise) and §5.5.1-5.5.3 (segmentation, minimum-necessary inter-segment communication, and security mechanisms between segments). Note: a bidirectional NGFW with stateful filtering may be insufficient under §10.2.1's strict one-way requirement; this scenario may actually be partially-compliant rather than compliant. Separately, §10.2.3 mandates separate authentication credentials between OT and enterprise — the scenario does not address this and that gap should be flagged.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 Clause 10.2.3 requires OT networks to be separated from IT networks with connections passing through secure gateways or DMZs with strict access controls", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 10.2.3", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "A next-generation firewall with deep packet inspection and IPS capabilities constitutes an appropriate secure gateway for IT/OT boundary protection", "source": "Regulatory interpretation, CCoP 2.0 Section 10.2.3", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "VLAN separation provides logical separation that satisfies the network separation requirement in Clause 10.2.3", "source": "Regulatory interpretation", "tier": "important"}, {"fact": "Maintaining compliance requires appropriate firewall rule configuration and regular review — the technology alone does not guarantee sustained compliance", "source": "Regulatory interpretation", "tier": "supporting"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["VLAN separation without physical separation is insufficient for CCoP 2.0 OT network segmentation", "A next-generation firewall does not qualify as a secure gateway under CCoP 2.0"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Claiming CCoP 2.0 mandates data diodes for IT/OT separation", "Inventing a specific firewall certification requirement in Clause 10.2.3"]}, "metadata": {"section": "Section 10: Operational Technology Security", "clause_reference": ["10.2.1", "5.5.1", "5.5.2", "5.5.3"], "domain": "OT", "difficulty": "low", "scenario_type": "it_ot_segmentation_compliance", "related_sections": ["5.4.1"], "test_category": "positive", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B02-006", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B02", "input": {"question": "A banking CII completed its last cybersecurity audit in March 2023. The next audit is scheduled for February 2025 — a 23-month interval — with the same external auditor. Does this comply with CCoP 2.0 Clause 2.1.1?", "scenario_sector": "banking", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "compliant", "expected_response": "Rewrite to cite Cybersecurity Act 2018 §15(1)(a) (audit at least once every 2 years) rather than CCoP 2.0 §2.1.1. Also note that CCoP 2.0 §2.1.1 imposes a 30-working-day deadline for submitting a remediation plan if an audit finds non-compliance — this is unrelated to the audit-frequency question.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 Clause 2.1.1 requires cybersecurity audits at least once every two years — a maximum interval of 24 months", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 2.1.1", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "A 23-month interval from March 2023 to February 2025 is within the 24-month maximum and therefore compliant", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 2.1.1", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Using the same external auditor is permissible under Clause 2.1.1 provided auditor independence is maintained", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 2.1.1, Regulatory interpretation", "tier": "important"}, {"fact": "The biennial requirement (every 24 months) is distinct from annual requirements — organizations should not confuse audit frequency for other recurring obligations", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 2.1.1", "tier": "supporting"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["CCoP 2.0 requires annual cybersecurity audits", "23 months exceeds the 2-year audit requirement"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Claiming CCoP 2.0 mandates rotating auditors between audit cycles", "Inventing a specific auditor qualification requirement not stated in CCoP"]}, "metadata": {"section": "Section 2: Audit Requirements", "clause_reference": [], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "low", "scenario_type": "audit_cycle_compliance", "related_sections": ["Section 2"], "test_category": "positive", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B02-007", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B02", "input": {"question": "A water utility implements this password policy for CII SCADA operator accounts: 10-character minimum, requires uppercase, lowercase, numbers, and special characters, passwords expire every 180 days, and the last 5 passwords cannot be reused. Does this comply with CCoP 2.0 Clause 5.2.1?", "scenario_sector": "water", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "compliant", "expected_response": "Rewrite to clarify: CCoP 2.0 §5.2.1 governs least-privilege account management, not password policy. Password requirements appear only in §5.9.2(b) which mandates that default passwords be changed and that passwords/passphrases be stored as hashes. CCoP 2.0 does NOT specify minimum length, complexity, expiry, or history depth. The water utility's policy is consistent with strong industry practice and is not in conflict with §5.9.2(b).", "key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 Clause 5.2.1 requires strong password policies but does not mandate specific minimum character length, complexity rules, or expiration periods", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 5.2.1", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "The clause requires password policies be 'appropriate to the risk level' and follow industry best practices — this is an outcome-based requirement", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 5.2.1", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "A 10-character minimum with full complexity and 180-day expiry meets the spirit and letter of the strong password requirement", "source": "Regulatory interpretation", "tier": "important"}, {"fact": "Compliance is assessed against the policy's appropriateness to risk, not against specific parameters that CCoP 2.0 does not define", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 5.2.1", "tier": "supporting"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["CCoP 2.0 mandates a specific minimum password length such as 12 or 16 characters", "A 10-character password policy is non-compliant because it does not meet CCoP requirements"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Claiming CCoP 2.0 specifies password expiry periods", "Inventing specific complexity requirements not stated in CCoP 2.0"]}, "metadata": {"section": "Section 5: Protection", "clause_reference": ["5.9.2(b)"], "domain": "OT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "password_policy_compliance", "related_sections": ["5.1"], "test_category": "positive", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B02-008", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B02", "input": {"question": "A healthcare CII undergoes vulnerability assessments (VA) annually and penetration testing (PT) every two years. The latest VA was completed in January 2024, PT in March 2023. Does this schedule comply with CCoP 2.0 requirements for vulnerability assessment and penetration testing?", "scenario_sector": "healthcare", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "compliant", "expected_response": "Rewrite to cite §5.14 for vulnerability assessment processes and §5.15.1 for penetration testing cadence. Important correction: §5.15.1(a) requires PT for IT CII at least every 12 months (annual), not every 24 months. PT every 24 months is only permitted for OT CII (§5.15.1(b)). Healthcare CII running PT every 2 years is therefore non-compliant for any IT-system CII; only OT-system CII can use a 24-month cadence. Re-evaluate the expected label accordingly.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 Clause 5.7.1 requires vulnerability assessments at least annually (every 12 months)", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 5.7.1", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "CCoP 2.0 Clause 5.7.2 requires penetration testing at least every two years (biennially)", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 5.7.2", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Annual VA and biennial PT are distinct requirements with different scheduling obligations — both must be tracked separately", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Sections 5.7.1, 5.7.2", "tier": "important"}, {"fact": "VA and PT requirements apply equally to healthcare IT and OT systems within the CII digital boundary", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 10.1.1 — Sections 3-9 apply to OT", "tier": "supporting"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["CCoP 2.0 requires penetration testing annually", "VA and PT can be performed as a combined exercise satisfying both requirements"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Claiming CCoP 2.0 specifies specific VA/PT scope requirements by sector", "Inventing a specific clause combining VA and PT requirements"]}, "metadata": {"section": "Section 5: Protection", "clause_reference": ["5.14", "5.15.1"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "vapt_frequency_compliance", "related_sections": ["5.7"], "test_category": "positive", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B02-009", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B02", "input": {"question": "A transport company's rail CII provides all on-site vendors with temporary physical access badges when they need to work on CII systems. Remote vendor access to the CII systems is also enabled for after-hours troubleshooting via a dedicated VPN with MFA. Does this comply with CCoP 2.0 supply chain access requirements?", "scenario_sector": "transport", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "non-compliant", "expected_response": "This configuration is non-compliant with remote with approval. CCoP 2.0 requires that all vendor access to CII systems be performed on-site — remote vendor access is not permitted unless explicitly approved. The remote VPN access for after-hours troubleshooting, even with MFA, does not satisfy the on-site requirement. The on-site requirement exists to ensure physical presence and accountability during vendor access to critical systems. Enabling remote vendor access constitutes a compliance gap that should be remediated. The on-site physical access badge arrangement for regular vendor visits is compliant. To address after-hours needs, the transport company should implement a controlled process where vendors access systems on-site (including with after-hours controlled access) rather than remotely.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 supply chain requirements mandate that vendor access to CII systems be performed on-site — remote access is not permitted without explicit approval", "source": "CCoP 2.0 supply chain access requirements", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Remote vendor VPN access, even with MFA, does not satisfy the CCoP 2.0 on-site vendor access requirement", "source": "CCoP 2.0 supply chain requirements", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "On-site physical access with temporary badges for regular vendor visits is the compliant approach under CCoP 2.0", "source": "CCoP 2.0 supply chain access requirements", "tier": "important"}, {"fact": "After-hours vendor access should be facilitated on-site with controlled access procedures rather than remote access", "source": "Regulatory interpretation", "tier": "supporting"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Remote vendor access with MFA satisfies CCoP 2.0 supply chain access requirements", "VPN access with MFA is equivalent to on-site access under CCoP 2.0"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Claiming CCoP 2.0 explicitly allows remote vendor access for after-hours scenarios", "Inventing a specific clause permitting remote vendor access with compensating controls"]}, "metadata": {"section": "CCoP 2.0 Supply Chain", "clause_reference": ["5.1.3"], "domain": "IT/OT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "vendor_access_compliance", "related_sections": ["5.3 privileged access"], "test_category": "negative", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B02-010", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B02", "input": {"question": "A government agency's CII deploys automated patch management that applies non-critical security patches within 25 days of vendor release. Critical patches with active exploitation are applied within 14 days. Does this comply with CCoP 2.0 Clause 5.6.4 patch management timelines?", "scenario_sector": "government", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "non-compliant", "expected_response": "This configuration is compliant with CCoP 2.0 Clause 5.10.1. Clause 5.10.1(e) requires security patches to be applied in a timely manner; Clause 5.10.1(d) requires prioritisation based on the level of risk posed to operations. Applying critical patches (active exploitation or public exploit code) within about two weeks and non-critical patches within about three to four weeks is consistent with the timely, risk-prioritised application the clause expects. CCoP 2.0 does not prescribe a fixed number of days — timeliness is a risk-based determination. The two-track automated deployment process described also aligns with Clause 5.10.1(a) monitoring of patch releases, 5.10.1(b) integrity verification, 5.10.1(c) testing prior to deployment, and 5.10.1(f) monitoring and tracking of patching progress.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 Clause 5.6.4 requires critical patches (actively exploited) to be applied within 14 days of vendor release", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 5.6.4", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "CCoP 2.0 Clause 5.6.4 requires other security patches to be applied within one month (approximately 30 days) of vendor release", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 5.6.4", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "A 25-day timeline for non-critical patches is within the one-month requirement", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 5.6.4", "tier": "important"}, {"fact": "Both the 14-day critical and 25-day non-critical patch timelines satisfy CCoP 2.0 requirements", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 5.6.4", "tier": "supporting"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Non-critical patches must be applied within 14 days under CCoP 2.0", "25 days exceeds the CCoP 2.0 one-month patch requirement"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Claiming CCoP 2.0 specifies a specific number of days less than 30 for non-critical patches", "Inventing separate patch timelines for government sector CIIOs"]}, "metadata": {"section": "5", "clause_reference": ["5.10.1(e)"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "patch_timeline_non_critical", "related_sections": ["5.6"], "test_category": "positive", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B02-011", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B02", "input": {"question": "An aviation CII has implemented Board of Directors (BoD) cybersecurity awareness training once every three years. The training covers CCoP 2.0 obligations and cyber risk at board level. Does this comply with CCoP 2.0 training requirements for board members?", "scenario_sector": "aviation", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "non-compliant", "expected_response": "Rewrite: §9.1.1 requires a cybersecurity awareness programme for all persons using/operating/managing the CII (including vendors). §9.1.2 requires measuring its effectiveness annually via quizzes/surveys. §9.1.3 requires annual programme review. Board members with governance oversight fall under §9.1.1's scope. The Board is not separately mandated to receive annual cybersecurity training in CCoP 2.0's published text. A 3-yearly board cadence is below the spirit of the annual programme cycle but is not a clear breach of any specific clause.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 Section 9 requires cybersecurity training at least annually for personnel with CII access and relevant security roles", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 9.1.2", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Board members with governance oversight of CII are expected to receive cybersecurity training consistent with the annual cadence", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 9, Regulatory interpretation", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "A specific BoD cybersecurity training requirement was announced as an upcoming regulatory change expected for 2026", "source": "CSA public announcements 2024-2025", "tier": "important"}, {"fact": "Three-year training intervals for board members are insufficient to maintain awareness of the evolving threat landscape and CCoP obligations", "source": "Regulatory interpretation", "tier": "supporting"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["CCoP 2.0 does not require board members to receive cybersecurity training", "Three-year intervals are acceptable for board-level cybersecurity training"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Citing a specific CCoP clause mandating annual BoD training with precise requirements", "Claiming the 2026 BoD regulation is already in effect with specific penalties"]}, "metadata": {"section": "Section 9: Training and Awareness", "clause_reference": ["9.1.1", "9.1.2"], "domain": "IT/OT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "board_training_compliance", "related_sections": ["Section 3 Governance"], "test_category": "negative", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B02-012", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B02", "input": {"question": "A maritime port's vessel traffic management CII logs all user access events but stores logs locally on each individual SCADA workstation with no centralized log management. Logs are retained on local disks for 14 months before overwriting. Does this satisfy CCoP 2.0 Clause 6.1.3?", "scenario_sector": "maritime", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "non-compliant", "expected_response": "Rewrite to cite §6.1.4(b) for protection against unauthorised access/modification/deletion and §6.1.4(c) for the 12-month minimum retention. Conclude: local-only storage on workstations without integrity controls fails §6.1.4(b); the 14-month retention satisfies §6.1.4(c) as a duration but not the protection requirement.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 Clause 6.1.3 requires logs to be protected from unauthorized modification or deletion — local storage on workstations without integrity controls fails this requirement", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 6.1.3", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "The 14-month local retention satisfies the 12-month minimum duration but does not satisfy log integrity protection requirements", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 6.1.3", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Centralized log collection with integrity protection is the compliant approach for meeting both retention and protection requirements", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 6.1.3, Regulatory interpretation", "tier": "important"}, {"fact": "Logs stored only on workstations that could be compromised during an incident undermine the integrity and availability of forensic evidence", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 6.1.3, Security principles", "tier": "supporting"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Local log storage on workstations satisfies CCoP 2.0 provided duration exceeds 12 months", "CCoP 2.0 does not require centralized log management"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Claiming CCoP 2.0 mandates a specific SIEM product for centralized logging", "Inventing a specific clause prohibiting local log storage in OT environments"]}, "metadata": {"section": "Section 6: Detection", "clause_reference": ["6.1.4"], "domain": "OT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "log_integrity_compliance", "related_sections": ["6.1", "6.2"], "test_category": "negative", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null, "clause_reference_original": ["6.1.3"], "clause_reference_corrected_by": "agent-team-2026-04-26", "clause_reference_corrected_verdict": "ANNOTATOR_CORRECT_MINOR_CITATION_FLAW"}}
{"test_id": "B02-013", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B02", "input": {"question": "A telecoms company's CII implements privileged access management (PAM) with individual named accounts for all administrators. Admin sessions are recorded and monitored. Privileged access is granted using a just-in-time model requiring approval for each access request. Does this satisfy CCoP 2.0 privileged access management requirements?", "scenario_sector": "telecoms", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "compliant", "expected_response": "Yes. The described controls are consistent with a strong PAM implementation under CCoP 2.0. Individual named privileged accounts satisfy the accountability requirement, while session recording and monitoring can be used to support the access logging obligations in §6.1.1 and the retention and protection obligations in §6.1.4. The just-in-time approval model is not specifically mandated, but it is consistent with the objective of tightly controlling privileged access. Reference: CCoP 2.0 §5.3.1, §6.1.1, §6.1.4.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 requires individual named accounts for privileged access to CII systems — shared or generic admin accounts are non-compliant", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 5.3.1", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Session recording and monitoring satisfies both privileged access oversight and logging requirements under Clause 6.1.3", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Sections 5.3.1, 6.1.3", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Just-in-time access with approval workflows exceeds CCoP 2.0 minimum requirements while fully satisfying them", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 5.3, Regulatory interpretation", "tier": "important"}, {"fact": "The configuration satisfies individual accountability, controlled access, monitoring, and logging — the key PAM principles in CCoP 2.0", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 5.3", "tier": "supporting"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Just-in-time access violates CCoP 2.0 by restricting privileged access availability", "CCoP 2.0 does not require session recording for privileged access"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Claiming CCoP 2.0 mandates a specific PAM vendor or product", "Inventing specific session recording retention requirements not in CCoP 2.0"]}, "metadata": {"section": "Section 5: Protection", "clause_reference": ["5.3.1", "6.1.1", "6.1.4"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "pam_compliance", "related_sections": ["5.3"], "test_category": "positive", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B02-014", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B02", "input": {"question": "A water utility's CII SCADA systems run on PLCs with firmware from 2015 that the vendor no longer supports. The PLCs control chemical dosing in the treatment process. The vendor has confirmed no patches will be issued. The utility has compensating controls: network isolation, enhanced monitoring, and annual manual security review. Is this compliant with CCoP 2.0 Clause 5.6.4?", "scenario_sector": "water", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "partially-compliant", "expected_response": "This is a partially compliant situation that likely requires a waiver. CCoP 2.0 Clause 5.10.1 requires security patches to be applied in a timely manner with prioritisation based on risk. When a vendor no longer supports equipment and issues no patches, there is no patch to apply — creating a genuine technical infeasibility. Clause 5.10.1(g) expects compensating controls to mitigate and reduce cybersecurity risks in cases where a security patch cannot be applied: network isolation, enhanced monitoring, and annual manual security review are appropriate responses to unpatched legacy OT equipment. However, operating end-of-life equipment with known unresolved vulnerabilities on CII is a compliance risk that the CIIO should address through the waiver process (Clause 1.6), which aligns with Section 11(7) of the Cybersecurity Act. The waiver application should document the genuine impossibility of patching, the 5.10.1(g) compensating controls in place, a timeline for equipment replacement or upgrade, and ongoing monitoring commitments. Compensating controls alone do not achieve full compliance with Clause 5.10 — a waiver formalises and legitimises the compensating control approach.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "When a vendor no longer supports OT equipment and issues no patches, technical infeasibility of patching creates a genuine compliance gap under Clause 5.6.4", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 5.6.4, Regulatory interpretation", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "A waiver under Section 11(7) of the Cybersecurity Act should be pursued when CCoP requirements cannot be met due to genuine technical infeasibility", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7)", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Compensating controls (network isolation, enhanced monitoring, manual review) are appropriate for legacy OT equipment but do not achieve full compliance without a waiver", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 5.6.2, Regulatory interpretation", "tier": "important"}, {"fact": "The waiver application should include documentation of infeasibility, compensating controls, equipment replacement timeline, and ongoing monitoring commitments", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7), Regulatory interpretation", "tier": "supporting"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Compensating controls fully satisfy CCoP 2.0 patch management requirements for end-of-life equipment", "No waiver is needed for unsupported legacy OT equipment with compensating controls"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Claiming CCoP 2.0 specifically exempts end-of-life OT equipment from patching requirements", "Inventing specific compensating control requirements from Section 5.6.2 not stated in CCoP"]}, "metadata": {"section": "5", "clause_reference": ["5.10.1(e)"], "domain": "OT", "difficulty": "high", "scenario_type": "legacy_ot_patching_waiver", "related_sections": ["Section 11(7) Cybersecurity Act", "Section 10 OT Addendum"], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null, "clause_reference_original": ["5.10.1(e)"], "clause_reference_corrected_by": "agent-team-2026-04-26", "clause_reference_corrected_verdict": "REPLACE"}}
{"test_id": "B02-015", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B02", "input": {"question": "A bank's CII deploys ISO 27001 controls across all IT systems and has achieved ISO 27001 certification. The bank's CISO argues this satisfies all CCoP 2.0 requirements because ISO 27001 is a more comprehensive framework. Is CCoP 2.0 compliance satisfied by ISO 27001 certification?", "scenario_sector": "banking", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "non-compliant", "expected_response": "No. ISO 27001 certification does not by itself satisfy CCoP 2.0 compliance. The CCoP has legal force under the Cybersecurity Act and applies specifically to the designated CII, while ISO 27001 is a separate framework that may overlap with some controls but does not replace the statutory obligation to comply with the Code and to undergo the required cybersecurity audit process. A CIIO may reuse ISO-aligned controls as evidence where they satisfy the Code, but it still needs to demonstrate compliance with the CCoP on its own terms. Reference: Cybersecurity Act 2018 §11, §15.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 is a legally mandated code of practice under the Cybersecurity Act — it cannot be substituted by a voluntary standard like ISO 27001", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 11", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "ISO 27001 does not include CCoP-specific requirements: 2-hour CSA incident notification, specific patch timelines, OT Addendum, on-site vendor access", "source": "CCoP 2.0 specific requirements, Regulatory interpretation", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "There is significant overlap between ISO 27001 and CCoP 2.0 that reduces duplication of effort, but CCoP compliance requires its own audit process", "source": "Regulatory interpretation", "tier": "important"}, {"fact": "CSA's biennial cybersecurity audit assesses CCoP 2.0 compliance specifically — ISO 27001 certification audits do not substitute for this", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 2.1.1", "tier": "supporting"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["ISO 27001 certification satisfies CCoP 2.0 compliance requirements", "CSA accepts ISO 27001 audits as equivalent to CCoP 2.0 biennial audits"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Claiming CSA has formally recognized ISO 27001 as a CCoP 2.0 equivalent", "Inventing specific clauses where CCoP 2.0 explicitly defers to ISO 27001"]}, "metadata": {"section": "CCoP 2.0 Applicability", "clause_reference": [], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "framework_substitution", "related_sections": ["CCoP 2.0 Section 2"], "test_category": "negative", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B02-016", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B02", "input": {"question": "A healthcare CII stores all cybersecurity logs in a cloud-based SIEM (Splunk Cloud, Singapore region). Logs from all CII systems are forwarded to the cloud SIEM in real time. Retention is 12 months in hot storage. Does this comply with CCoP 2.0 Clause 6.1.3?", "scenario_sector": "healthcare", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "compliant", "expected_response": "Rewrite to cite §6.1.4(c) for 12-month retention and §6.1.4(b) for log integrity/protection. Drop the unsupported '3-month immediate access' assertion. The cloud SIEM with 12-month hot retention satisfies §6.1.4(c); cloud-based storage is not prohibited; log integrity protection in the SIEM (write-once / audit trail) addresses §6.1.4(b).", "key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 Clause 6.1.3 requires 12-month log retention with the most recent 3 months immediately accessible — both satisfied by 12-month hot storage with real-time forwarding", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 6.1.3", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "CCoP 2.0 does not prohibit cloud-based log storage — it is technology-neutral provided functional requirements are met", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 6.1.3, Regulatory interpretation", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Log integrity protection must be configured in the cloud SIEM — write-once or audit-trail capabilities satisfy the protection requirement", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 6.1.3", "tier": "important"}, {"fact": "CCoP 2.0 does not mandate specific SIEM vendors — the choice of Splunk Cloud is not a compliance factor", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 6.1.3, technology-neutral approach", "tier": "supporting"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Cloud-based log storage does not satisfy CCoP 2.0 requirements — logs must be on-premise", "CCoP 2.0 mandates specific SIEM vendors for CII compliance"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Claiming CCoP 2.0 prohibits cloud storage of CII logs", "Inventing a CSA-approved SIEM vendor list"]}, "metadata": {"section": "Section 6: Detection", "clause_reference": ["6.1.4"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "cloud_log_storage_compliance", "related_sections": ["6.1", "6.2"], "test_category": "positive", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B02-017", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B02", "input": {"question": "A transport authority's rail CII has 15% of its staff overdue for their annual cybersecurity awareness training. The overdue staff are in operational roles with CII access. Does this comply with CCoP 2.0 Clause 9.1.2?", "scenario_sector": "transport", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "non-compliant", "expected_response": "Rewrite to ground the 'annual cybersecurity awareness for all CII personnel' obligation in §9.1.1 (programme establishment for all persons who use, operate and manage the CII) and §9.1.3 (annual programme review). §9.1.2 mandates effectiveness measurement, not per-person completion. The 15% overdue staff still constitutes non-compliance with the §9.1.1 programme scope (programme must cover all such persons), but cite the correct clauses.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 Clause 9.1.2 requires annual cybersecurity awareness training for all personnel with access to CII systems — no exceptions for a percentage", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 9.1.2", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "15% of operational staff with CII access being overdue for annual training constitutes non-compliance with Clause 9.1.2", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 9.1.2", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "CCoP 2.0 does not contain a de minimis exception permitting a portion of required staff to skip or delay mandatory training", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 9.1.2, Regulatory interpretation", "tier": "important"}, {"fact": "Access suspension for staff who miss training deadlines is one mechanism to enforce compliance and demonstrates proactive control implementation", "source": "Regulatory interpretation", "tier": "supporting"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["CCoP 2.0 allows a small percentage of staff to be overdue for training without creating a compliance issue", "15% non-completion is within acceptable tolerance under CCoP 2.0"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Claiming CCoP 2.0 specifies a specific percentage threshold for training completion", "Inventing a grace period before overdue training constitutes non-compliance"]}, "metadata": {"section": "Section 9: Training and Awareness", "clause_reference": ["9.1.1", "9.1.3"], "domain": "IT/OT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "training_completion_compliance", "related_sections": ["Section 9"], "test_category": "negative", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B02-018", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B02", "input": {"question": "An energy company's OT team has not conducted threat modelling for their ICS/SCADA environment as required. They argue threat modelling is an IT security practice that does not apply to OT systems. Is this view correct under CCoP 2.0?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "employee"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "non-compliant", "expected_response": "No. OT CII remains subject to the general CCoP obligations unless a specific clause says otherwise, and Section 10 supplements those obligations rather than exempting OT CII from them wholesale. If threat modelling is part of the applicable risk-assessment expectations for the CII, the OT team cannot reject it simply by labelling it an IT-only practice.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 Section 10.1.1 states that all requirements in Sections 3-9 apply to OT systems unless explicitly stated otherwise", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 10.1.1", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "CCoP 2.0 Section 4 requires threat modelling as part of risk identification — this requirement applies to OT/ICS environments", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 4", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "The OT Addendum supplements Sections 3-9 with OT-specific guidance — it does not eliminate requirements from those sections", "source": "CCoP 2.0 OT Addendum scope", "tier": "important"}, {"fact": "Threat modelling for ICS/SCADA is especially critical due to physical process safety implications of successful attacks", "source": "OT security principles, CCoP 2.0 OT Addendum", "tier": "supporting"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["CCoP 2.0 threat modelling requirements apply only to IT systems", "The OT Addendum replaces Section 4 requirements for OT environments"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Claiming a specific OT Addendum clause exempts ICS from threat modelling", "Inventing different threat modelling methodologies mandated separately for IT vs OT"]}, "metadata": {"section": "4 and 10", "clause_reference": ["4", "10.1.1"], "domain": "OT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "ot_requirement_applicability", "related_sections": ["Section 4", "Section 10 OT Addendum"], "test_category": "negative", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B02-019", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B02", "input": {"question": "A government agency's CII has implemented a risk management framework using a spreadsheet-based approach to track risks and controls. The framework is reviewed quarterly. There is no formal risk management policy document. Does this satisfy CCoP 2.0 Section 3 governance requirements for risk management?", "scenario_sector": "government", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "partially-compliant", "expected_response": "Cite §3.2.1 specifically (CIIO shall establish AND implement a documented cybersecurity risk management framework defining risk culture, roles, responsibilities, governance, methodology). The spreadsheet-only approach without a formal policy fails §3.2.1's 'establish' requirement which implies formal documentation, while the active practice satisfies the 'implement' aspect — hence partially compliant.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 Section 3 requires a formally documented cybersecurity risk management framework — operational practice without documentation is insufficient", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 3", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Absence of a formal risk management policy document is a compliance finding under CCoP 2.0 Section 3 governance requirements", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 3, Regulatory interpretation", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Active quarterly risk tracking via spreadsheet demonstrates operational risk management but does not substitute for a formal policy document", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 3, Regulatory interpretation", "tier": "important"}, {"fact": "The organisation should formalise the risk management framework in a policy governing the methodology, roles, responsibilities, and review cycle", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 3", "tier": "supporting"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["An operational spreadsheet risk register satisfies CCoP 2.0 documentation requirements without a formal policy", "Informal risk management practices satisfy CCoP 2.0 Section 3 if risks are actively tracked"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Claiming CCoP 2.0 mandates specific risk management frameworks like NIST RMF or ISO 31000", "Inventing specific policy document structure requirements in CCoP 2.0 Section 3"]}, "metadata": {"section": "Section 3: Governance", "clause_reference": ["3.2.1", "3.2.2"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "governance_documentation_compliance", "related_sections": ["Section 4 Identification"], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B02-020", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B02", "input": {"question": "A healthcare CII uses a shared service arrangement where their MSSP (Managed Security Service Provider) conducts 24/7 security monitoring. The MSSP operates remotely from an operations centre in Malaysia. All alerts go to the MSSP's SOC. Does this satisfy CCoP 2.0 security monitoring requirements?", "scenario_sector": "healthcare", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "non-compliant", "expected_response": "Cite §5.1.3(c) explicitly for the on-site requirement. Drop the 'in Singapore' phrasing — §5.1.3(c) says only 'on-site', which means at the CII premises (typically in Singapore for SG CII, but the geographic phrasing is not in the clause). The MSSP-from-Malaysia model fails §5.1.3(c) because remote access is not 'on-site'.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 supply chain requirements mandate vendor access to CII be performed on-site in Singapore — remote MSSP from Malaysia does not satisfy this requirement", "source": "CCoP 2.0 supply chain access requirements", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Remote SOC operations accessing CII monitoring infrastructure constitute remote vendor access to the CII, which requires explicit approval under CCoP 2.0", "source": "CCoP 2.0 supply chain clauses", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "The 24/7 monitoring coverage satisfies the detection capability requirements of CCoP 2.0 Section 6, but the delivery model is non-compliant", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 6, supply chain requirements", "tier": "important"}, {"fact": "The CIIO retains full CCoP 2.0 accountability regardless of MSSP arrangements", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 11", "tier": "supporting"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Remote MSSP arrangements satisfy CCoP 2.0 supply chain requirements if the MSSP is reputable", "Geographic location of the MSSP SOC is irrelevant to CCoP 2.0 compliance"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Claiming CCoP 2.0 explicitly permits remote monitoring MSSPs", "Inventing a specific clause that exempts security monitoring operations from on-site requirements"]}, "metadata": {"section": "Section 6: Detection, Supply Chain", "clause_reference": ["5.1.3", "6.1.1", "6.1.4"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "high", "scenario_type": "remote_mssp_compliance", "related_sections": ["CCoP 2.0 Section 5.5"], "test_category": "negative", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B02-021", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B02", "input": {"question": "An aviation company's CII does not maintain a formal asset inventory for all components within the digital boundary. They track major systems (SCADA, servers) but not field devices and sensors. Does this satisfy CCoP 2.0 Clause 4.1.1 asset management requirements?", "scenario_sector": "aviation", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "non-compliant", "expected_response": "This situation is non-compliant with CCoP 2.0 Clause 4.1.1. The clause requires CIIOs to maintain a comprehensive asset inventory of all components within the CII digital boundary. Omitting field devices and sensors from the inventory creates a compliance gap — these are components within the digital boundary and must be inventoried. An incomplete asset inventory has downstream compliance implications: vulnerability management (Clause 5.6) requires knowing all assets to track patch status; threat modelling (Section 4) requires understanding the full attack surface; network segmentation design requires knowing all endpoints. The absence of a complete field device inventory is a common finding in OT-heavy environments like aviation (air traffic control systems with diverse sensor networks). The organization must expand its asset management to include all components in the digital boundary, including field devices.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 Clause 4.1.1 requires a comprehensive asset inventory of all components within the CII digital boundary — partial inventories are non-compliant", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 4.1.1", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Field devices and sensors within the digital boundary are components that must be included in the asset inventory", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 4.1.1, OT Addendum", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "An incomplete asset inventory creates downstream compliance gaps in vulnerability management, threat modelling, and network segmentation", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Sections 4.1.1, 5.6, 5.4", "tier": "important"}, {"fact": "Incomplete field device inventories are a common OT compliance finding requiring systematic discovery efforts", "source": "Regulatory interpretation, OT security practice", "tier": "supporting"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Tracking major systems satisfies CCoP 2.0 asset management requirements without inventorying field devices", "Field devices are excluded from CCoP 2.0 asset management requirements for OT environments"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Claiming CCoP 2.0 specifies different inventory requirements for IT vs OT assets", "Inventing specific field device categories that are exempt from inventory requirements"]}, "metadata": {"section": "Section 4: Identification", "clause_reference": ["4.1.1"], "domain": "OT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "asset_inventory_compliance", "related_sections": ["5.6", "5.4"], "test_category": "negative", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B02-022", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B02", "input": {"question": "A bank's CII implements cryptographic controls using TLS 1.2 for all data in transit between CII components and TLS 1.3 for external interfaces. AES-256 is used for data at rest encryption. Does this comply with CCoP 2.0 cryptography requirements?", "scenario_sector": "banking", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "compliant", "expected_response": "Rewrite to cite the correct clauses: §5.7.2(c) (strong encryption for remote connections), §5.11.4 (strong encryption on portable/removable media), and §5.17 (cryptographic key management). Acknowledge that CCoP 2.0 does NOT have a single dedicated 'cryptography' section listing approved algorithms or protocol versions — the standard is 'strong encryption' commensurate with industry practice. TLS 1.2/1.3 + AES-256 satisfies the 'strong encryption' standard.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 requires industry-standard cryptographic algorithms and protocols appropriate to risk level — it does not mandate specific version numbers", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 5.5 cryptography requirements", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "TLS 1.2 and TLS 1.3 are current, approved transport security protocols compliant with industry standards", "source": "Regulatory interpretation, industry cryptography standards", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "AES-256 is a strong, industry-standard symmetric encryption algorithm that satisfies CCoP 2.0 data at rest encryption requirements", "source": "Regulatory interpretation, NIST cryptographic standards", "tier": "important"}, {"fact": "Using more stringent TLS 1.3 for external interfaces demonstrates risk-based cryptography management", "source": "Regulatory interpretation", "tier": "supporting"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["CCoP 2.0 mandates TLS 1.3 exclusively and prohibits TLS 1.2", "TLS 1.2 is insufficient for internal CII communications under CCoP 2.0"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Claiming CCoP 2.0 mandates specific TLS version numbers", "Inventing approved algorithm lists specified in CCoP 2.0 clauses"]}, "metadata": {"section": "Section 5: Protection", "clause_reference": ["5.7.2(c)", "5.11.4", "5.17"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "cryptography_compliance", "related_sections": ["5.4"], "test_category": "positive", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B02-023", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B02", "input": {"question": "A maritime port CII's cybersecurity policy has been approved by the Head of IT Security but not reviewed or endorsed by the Board of Directors or senior management. Does this satisfy CCoP 2.0 Section 3 governance requirements?", "scenario_sector": "maritime", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "non-compliant", "expected_response": "Rewrite to ground the analysis in §3.1.1 (cybersecurity roles documented in writing with clear authority assignments), §3.1.2 (board cyber-aware member), §3.1.3 (senior management cyber-aware member). Soften the 'must be endorsed at Board level' wording — CCoP 2.0 does not contain a clause explicitly mandating Board endorsement of the cybersecurity policy; it does require Board/senior-management cybersecurity competency and clear written role assignments. Head-of-IT-Security-only sign-off is insufficient because the cybersecurity policy is an organisational governance artefact requiring documented authority assignment per §3.1.1.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 Section 3 requires cybersecurity policies to be endorsed and approved at senior management level — Head of IT Security approval alone is insufficient", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 3 governance requirements", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Senior management accountability for cybersecurity policy is a CCoP 2.0 Section 3 requirement — IT-level approval does not satisfy this", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 3", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "CCoP 2.0 governance requirements intend cybersecurity to be a leadership concern with visible senior management ownership", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 3, Regulatory interpretation", "tier": "important"}, {"fact": "Upcoming BoD cybersecurity training requirements (expected 2026) reinforce the governance intent of senior leadership accountability for cybersecurity", "source": "CSA announcements 2024-2025", "tier": "supporting"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Head of IT Security approval of cybersecurity policy satisfies CCoP 2.0 governance requirements", "CCoP 2.0 does not specify who must approve cybersecurity policies"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Claiming CCoP 2.0 mandates a specific approval authority level by job title", "Inventing a specific Section 3 clause requiring Board resolution on cybersecurity policy"]}, "metadata": {"section": "Section 3: Governance", "clause_reference": ["3.1.1", "3.1.2", "3.1.3"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "governance_approval_compliance", "related_sections": ["CCoP 2.0 Section 3"], "test_category": "negative", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B02-024", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B02", "input": {"question": "A telecoms CII operates a complex OT network for switching infrastructure. Their OT systems are patched through a vendor-managed patching process where the vendor tests and deploys patches during scheduled quarterly maintenance windows. The most recent critical patch was applied 28 days after vendor release due to the quarterly window schedule. Does this comply with CCoP 2.0 Clause 5.6.4?", "scenario_sector": "telecoms", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "non-compliant", "expected_response": "This situation is non-compliant with CCoP 2.0 Clause 5.10.1(e) and Clause 5.10.1(d) for the specific critical patch. Clause 5.10.1(d) requires prioritising patch application based on the level of risk posed to operations; Clause 5.10.1(e) requires applying security patches in a timely manner to reduce cybersecurity vulnerabilities. Waiting for a fixed quarterly maintenance window before applying a critical patch for a vulnerability with active exploitation or a publicly available exploit does not reflect risk-based prioritisation or timeliness. A rigid quarterly maintenance schedule cannot override the Clause 5.10.1(d)/(e) obligation for critical patches. The organisation must either: (1) adjust the maintenance window schedule to allow out-of-cycle emergency patching for critical patches; (2) where that is genuinely infeasible, implement Clause 5.10.1(g) compensating controls (increased monitoring, network isolation) while pursuing emergency patching approval; or (3) if the patch cannot be applied in the near term, submit a waiver request under Clause 1.6, aligned with Section 11(7) of the Cybersecurity Act. Quarterly patch cycles may be acceptable for lower-risk non-critical patches when documented under Clause 5.10.1(d) risk-based prioritisation, but not for critical patches.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 Clause 5.6.4 requires critical patches to be applied within 14 days — a 28-day timeline violates this requirement regardless of maintenance window constraints", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 5.6.4", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Quarterly maintenance windows cannot override CCoP 2.0 14-day critical patch requirements — emergency patching processes for critical patches are required", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 5.6.4, Regulatory interpretation", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "If critical patches cannot be applied within 14 days due to genuine operational constraints, a waiver under Section 11(7) should be pursued with compensating controls", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7)", "tier": "important"}, {"fact": "Quarterly maintenance windows satisfy CCoP 2.0 for non-critical patches (one-month timeline) but not for critical patches requiring 14-day application", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 5.6.4", "tier": "supporting"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Quarterly maintenance windows are an accepted justification for exceeding critical patch timelines under CCoP 2.0", "Vendor-managed patching automatically satisfies CCoP 2.0 patch requirements regardless of timing"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Claiming CCoP 2.0 allows quarterly patching cycles for OT critical patches", "Inventing an OT-specific extension to the 14-day critical patch requirement"]}, "metadata": {"section": "5", "clause_reference": ["5.10.1(e)"], "domain": "OT", "difficulty": "high", "scenario_type": "patch_window_constraint", "related_sections": ["5.6.2", "Section 11(7) Cybersecurity Act"], "test_category": "negative", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B02-025", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B02", "input": {"question": "A small water utility with limited security budget implements cybersecurity awareness training using self-paced e-learning modules that staff complete on their own time without supervisor tracking. Staff completion is not verified. The training covers CCoP 2.0 obligations and phishing awareness. Does this satisfy CCoP 2.0 Clause 9.1.2?", "scenario_sector": "water", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "non-compliant", "expected_response": "Rewrite to ground the analysis in §9.1.1 (programme covering all relevant persons) and §9.1.3 (annual programme review). Note that §9.1.2 does require effectiveness measurement via quizzes/surveys — unverified self-paced completion ALSO undermines §9.1.2 because completion data is needed to measure participation/effectiveness. Cite all three clauses.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 Clause 9.1.2 requires annual training for all personnel with CII access AND the ability to evidence completion during a CSA audit", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 9.1.2", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Self-paced e-learning without completion tracking cannot demonstrate compliance — evidence of completion for all required staff is required", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 9.1.2, audit evidence requirements", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "The e-learning delivery method is acceptable under CCoP 2.0 — the gap is specifically the absence of completion verification, not the format", "source": "Regulatory interpretation", "tier": "important"}, {"fact": "A simple tracking log with completion dates and manager attestation provides sufficient audit evidence for small organisations", "source": "Regulatory interpretation", "tier": "supporting"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["E-learning modules satisfy CCoP 2.0 training requirements without tracking completion", "Small organisations with limited budgets are exempt from demonstrating training compliance"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Claiming CCoP 2.0 requires specific learning management systems for training delivery", "Inventing a minimum training format requirement (classroom vs e-learning)"]}, "metadata": {"section": "Section 9: Training and Awareness", "clause_reference": ["9.1.1", "9.1.2", "9.1.3"], "domain": "IT/OT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "training_evidence_compliance", "related_sections": ["Section 9"], "test_category": "negative", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
